1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,771
Gabon, West Africa.

2
00:00:01,771 --> 00:00:09,115
Deep inside a uranium mine, geologists find rock that has already been through a nuclear reactor.

3
00:00:09,115 --> 00:00:10,781
Two billion years ago.

4
00:00:10,781 --> 00:00:11,916
Before animals.

5
00:00:11,916 --> 00:00:14,566
Before anyone could have built one.

6
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:18,504
So who built the world's first nuclear reactor?

7
00:00:18,504 --> 00:00:19,833
Ancient engineers?

8
00:00:19,833 --> 00:00:21,311
Visitors from space?

9
00:00:21,311 --> 00:00:22,566
Or nobody at all?

10
00:00:23,033 --> 00:00:26,852
Tonight this is a cold case, and the evidence is atoms.

11
00:00:26,852 --> 00:00:32,200
We read isotopes like fingerprints, score every claim, and deliver a verdict.

12
00:00:32,666 --> 00:00:33,390
Facts first.

13
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:34,475
Theories labelled.

14
00:00:34,475 --> 00:00:38,333
And the rock does not lie, though it takes some careful reading.

15
00:00:38,799 --> 00:00:40,191
Case file open.

16
00:00:40,191 --> 00:00:42,233
Location: Oklo, Gabon.

17
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:47,607
Chapter one.

18
00:00:47,607 --> 00:00:49,118
The missing uranium.

19
00:00:49,118 --> 00:00:53,426
May, nineteen seventy-two, a French plant at Pierrelatte.

20
00:00:53,426 --> 00:00:58,566
Technicians are checking a routine sample of uranium ore from Gabon.

21
00:00:59,033 --> 00:01:01,521
Natural uranium is a fixed mix.

22
00:01:01,521 --> 00:01:05,132
Almost all of it is uranium two thirty-eight.

23
00:01:05,132 --> 00:01:11,633
Just point seven two percent is uranium two thirty-five, the isotope that splits.

24
00:01:12,099 --> 00:01:17,604
That ratio is the same in moon rock, in meteorites, in every mine on Earth.

25
00:01:17,604 --> 00:01:20,466
It is practically a constant of nature.

26
00:01:20,933 --> 00:01:24,322
The Gabon sample read point six zero percent.

27
00:01:24,322 --> 00:01:29,066
Roughly a sixth of the uranium two thirty-five was simply gone.

28
00:01:29,533 --> 00:01:31,157
They assumed a mistake.

29
00:01:31,157 --> 00:01:32,570
They measured again.

30
00:01:32,570 --> 00:01:33,276
And again.

31
00:01:33,276 --> 00:01:37,866
Some ore from the mine came in as low as point four four percent.

32
00:01:38,333 --> 00:01:40,395
This was not just curiosity.

33
00:01:40,395 --> 00:01:45,477
Uranium bookkeeping is how the world tracks nuclear weapons material.

34
00:01:45,477 --> 00:01:49,233
Missing uranium means someone might be stealing it.

35
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:54,054
Investigators followed the ore trail back to a single place.

36
00:01:54,054 --> 00:01:58,700
A mine near the town of Oklo, in the Franceville basin of Gabon.

37
00:01:59,166 --> 00:02:00,951
They did not find thieves.

38
00:02:00,951 --> 00:02:03,492
They found rock that had been burned.

39
00:02:03,492 --> 00:02:08,366
Something had used up that uranium long before any human held a shovel.

40
00:02:08,833 --> 00:02:16,535
The French Atomic Energy Commission made the announcement on the twenty-fifth of September, nineteen seventy-two.

41
00:02:16,535 --> 00:02:19,466
The mine contained fossil nuclear reactors.

42
00:02:19,933 --> 00:02:20,743
Chapter two.

43
00:02:20,743 --> 00:02:21,891
The fingerprints.

44
00:02:21,891 --> 00:02:27,900
A claim that big needs proof, and a reactor leaves a signature that is very hard to fake.

45
00:02:28,366 --> 00:02:34,807
When uranium two thirty-five splits, it breaks into smaller atoms called fission products.

46
00:02:34,807 --> 00:02:38,099
Those leftovers are the ash of a nuclear fire.

47
00:02:38,566 --> 00:02:42,413
Scientists analysed neodymium in the Oklo rock.

48
00:02:42,413 --> 00:02:49,617
Natural neodymium has about twenty-seven percent of the isotope neodymium one forty-two.

49
00:02:49,617 --> 00:02:51,500
Oklo had less than six.

50
00:02:51,966 --> 00:02:56,005
Instead, the neodymium matched the pattern left by fission.

51
00:02:56,005 --> 00:02:58,402
It was not the fingerprint of rock.

52
00:02:58,402 --> 00:03:00,866
It was the fingerprint of a reactor.

53
00:03:01,333 --> 00:03:03,659
Ruthenium told the same story.

54
00:03:03,659 --> 00:03:09,008
One isotope, ruthenium ninety-nine, is normally about twelve percent.

55
00:03:09,008 --> 00:03:15,133
At Oklo it reached nearly thirty, the product of fission and radioactive decay.

56
00:03:15,599 --> 00:03:18,862
Nobody carries fission ash into a mine by hand.

57
00:03:18,862 --> 00:03:24,000
It formed right where it is found, in the same grains, in the same layers.

58
00:03:24,466 --> 00:03:26,330
And there is a second clue.

59
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:31,851
Uranium two thirty-four, which does not come from fission, was perfectly normal.

60
00:03:31,851 --> 00:03:35,233
That is exactly what a natural reaction predicts.

61
00:03:35,699 --> 00:03:38,484
Three independent lines of evidence.

62
00:03:38,484 --> 00:03:43,821
Depleted uranium, fission ash, and untouched uranium two thirty-four.

63
00:03:43,821 --> 00:03:45,599
All point the same way.

64
00:03:46,066 --> 00:03:49,095
The reactor theory had even been predicted.

65
00:03:49,095 --> 00:03:56,000
In nineteen fifty-six, a chemist named Paul Kuroda had argued such a thing could happen in nature.

66
00:03:56,466 --> 00:03:57,418
Chapter three.

67
00:03:57,418 --> 00:03:59,323
How a reactor builds itself.

68
00:03:59,323 --> 00:04:06,599
A chain reaction needs fuel, and it needs something to slow the neutrons down so they can split more atoms.

69
00:04:07,066 --> 00:04:11,330
In a modern plant, that slowing is done by water or graphite.

70
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:15,733
At Oklo, it was plain groundwater seeping through cracked rock.

71
00:04:16,199 --> 00:04:19,919
But that only works if the uranium is rich enough.

72
00:04:19,919 --> 00:04:22,745
Today natural uranium is far too weak.

73
00:04:22,745 --> 00:04:25,199
Two billion years ago it was not.

74
00:04:25,666 --> 00:04:30,476
Uranium two thirty-five decays faster than uranium two thirty-eight.

75
00:04:30,476 --> 00:04:37,266
Wind the clock back and it was about three percent, close to the fuel in a modern power station.

76
00:04:37,733 --> 00:04:39,942
There was another requirement.

77
00:04:39,942 --> 00:04:40,457
Oxygen.

78
00:04:40,457 --> 00:04:47,600
Oxygen in the air and water dissolves uranium and carries it, so it can collect in rich deposits.

79
00:04:48,066 --> 00:04:52,861
Oxygen had only recently been pumped into the atmosphere by tiny bacteria.

80
00:04:52,861 --> 00:04:57,266
A few hundred million years earlier, this could never have happened.

81
00:04:57,733 --> 00:04:59,598
So the timing is perfect.

82
00:04:59,598 --> 00:05:02,358
Enough oxygen to concentrate the ore.

83
00:05:02,358 --> 00:05:05,492
Enough uranium two thirty-five to fuel it.

84
00:05:05,492 --> 00:05:08,699
And water flowing in to start the reaction.

85
00:05:09,166 --> 00:05:11,945
Then came the genius of nature's design.

86
00:05:11,945 --> 00:05:14,932
When the reaction ran, it heated the water.

87
00:05:14,932 --> 00:05:19,100
The water boiled away, and without it, the reaction stopped.

88
00:05:19,566 --> 00:05:23,240
Cool down, water returns, the reaction restarts.

89
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:29,133
A self-regulating reactor with no operator, no control rods, and no designer.

90
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:30,433
Chapter four.

91
00:05:30,433 --> 00:05:31,331
The heartbeat.

92
00:05:31,331 --> 00:05:35,180
How do you know how a reactor behaved two billion years ago?

93
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:37,233
You look for the gas it trapped.

94
00:05:37,699 --> 00:05:40,472
Fission produces xenon, a heavy gas.

95
00:05:40,472 --> 00:05:47,633
At Oklo, researchers found it locked inside tiny crystals of aluminium phosphate in the rock.

96
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:53,805
All five xenon isotopes were there, in proportions that only fission can make.

97
00:05:53,805 --> 00:05:56,000
And their ratios hold a clock.

98
00:05:56,466 --> 00:05:58,768
Some xenon forms later than others.

99
00:05:58,768 --> 00:06:03,833
By reading what was trapped, scientists worked out the rhythm of the reactor.

100
00:06:04,300 --> 00:06:06,534
Roughly thirty minutes of fission.

101
00:06:06,534 --> 00:06:10,937
Then about two and a half hours of cooling while water seeped back.

102
00:06:10,937 --> 00:06:13,566
A cycle of about three hours, repeating.

103
00:06:14,033 --> 00:06:17,513
Over and over, for hundreds of thousands of years.

104
00:06:17,513 --> 00:06:22,733
A natural geyser of nuclear fire, quietly pulsing under the African ground.

105
00:06:23,199 --> 00:06:24,383
And it was gentle.

106
00:06:24,383 --> 00:06:30,566
The average output stayed under a hundred kilowatts, about what a few dozen kettles would use.

107
00:06:31,033 --> 00:06:40,300
Across sixteen separate zones the pattern repeated, each a seam of ore only centimetres thick, each reaching criticality on its own.

108
00:06:40,766 --> 00:06:43,991
This was no accident of one lucky pocket.

109
00:06:43,991 --> 00:06:47,766
It was geology repeating a recipe sixteen times.

110
00:06:48,233 --> 00:06:49,165
Chapter five.

111
00:06:49,165 --> 00:06:50,098
The suspects.

112
00:06:50,098 --> 00:06:58,133
Now the cold case gets interesting, because online, Oklo has become a favourite of the ancient-technology crowd.

113
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:01,410
The first suspect: visitors from space.

114
00:07:01,410 --> 00:07:08,833
The claim goes that Oklo was a reactor built by an advanced civilisation, and the waste is their trash.

115
00:07:09,300 --> 00:07:17,366
A second version says a lost human civilisation built it, an ancient nuclear power plant that predates everything we know.

116
00:07:17,833 --> 00:07:21,440
Suspect one, the engineers, needs a body of evidence.

117
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:26,000
Where are the tools, the buildings, the control systems, the bones?

118
00:07:26,466 --> 00:07:27,560
There are none.

119
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:31,646
At Oklo, there is only natural rock with natural cracks.

120
00:07:31,646 --> 00:07:35,366
No metal, no walls, no pipes, nothing manufactured.

121
00:07:35,833 --> 00:07:40,337
And two billion years ago, the Earth had no animals at all.

122
00:07:40,337 --> 00:07:41,863
Life meant microbes.

123
00:07:41,863 --> 00:07:44,153
No hands, no brains, no tools.

124
00:07:44,153 --> 00:07:45,833
Nothing to build with.

125
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:51,919
Even the oldest human civilisations are only about five thousand years old.

126
00:07:51,919 --> 00:07:55,066
Oklo is four hundred thousand times older.

127
00:07:55,533 --> 00:07:58,401
A designed reactor would also be better.

128
00:07:58,401 --> 00:08:02,488
It would be steady, and it would carry waste away safely.

129
00:08:02,488 --> 00:08:05,500
Oklo is messy, intermittent and low power.

130
00:08:05,966 --> 00:08:10,575
Everything that makes Oklo special is explained without engineers.

131
00:08:10,575 --> 00:08:14,066
The only mystery left is why nobody saw it sooner.

132
00:08:14,533 --> 00:08:15,440
Chapter six.

133
00:08:15,440 --> 00:08:18,616
The strange part is the rest of the world.

134
00:08:18,616 --> 00:08:19,750
Why only Gabon?

135
00:08:19,750 --> 00:08:22,699
Why has no second Oklo ever been found?

136
00:08:23,166 --> 00:08:25,985
Because it needed everything at once.

137
00:08:25,985 --> 00:08:27,738
A rich, thick ore body.

138
00:08:27,738 --> 00:08:29,490
Oxygen-carried uranium.

139
00:08:29,490 --> 00:08:30,557
Flowing water.

140
00:08:30,557 --> 00:08:34,366
And time, during a narrow window of Earth history.

141
00:08:34,833 --> 00:08:39,398
As uranium two thirty-five decayed away, the window closed.

142
00:08:39,398 --> 00:08:43,500
Today, no natural deposit can ever go critical again.

143
00:08:43,966 --> 00:08:48,231
Researchers have checked other very old uranium deposits.

144
00:08:48,231 --> 00:08:51,075
Some show hints, but none comes close.

145
00:08:51,075 --> 00:08:54,966
Oklo is the one place where the conditions lined up.

146
00:08:55,433 --> 00:08:58,621
In science, rare is not the same as impossible.

147
00:08:58,621 --> 00:09:03,233
A natural event with the right ingredients does not need a designer.

148
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:06,388
And Oklo has taught us something else.

149
00:09:06,388 --> 00:09:12,966
Physicists used the isotope ratios to test whether the laws of nature have changed over time.

150
00:09:13,433 --> 00:09:19,650
So far, the fine-structure constant looks remarkably stable across two billion years.

151
00:09:19,650 --> 00:09:23,600
The fossil reactor is also a time capsule for physics.

152
00:09:24,066 --> 00:09:25,058
Chapter seven.

153
00:09:25,058 --> 00:09:26,334
The lesson for us.

154
00:09:26,334 --> 00:09:31,366
Nuclear waste worries everyone, because it stays dangerous for so long.

155
00:09:31,833 --> 00:09:40,466
At Oklo, plutonium and fission products stayed within metres of where they formed for nearly two billion years, held in the rock.

156
00:09:40,933 --> 00:09:44,681
Engineers study the site to design deep waste storage.

157
00:09:44,681 --> 00:09:48,500
Nature already ran the experiment, and the ground held.

158
00:09:48,966 --> 00:09:51,311
That does not make storage easy.

159
00:09:51,311 --> 00:09:58,566
But it shows that stable geology can lock atoms away longer than any human civilisation has lasted.

160
00:09:59,033 --> 00:10:01,108
The mine itself is now closed.

161
00:10:01,108 --> 00:10:06,433
The reactor zones were largely mined out, but a few were preserved for study.

162
00:10:06,899 --> 00:10:08,950
Time for the forensic report.

163
00:10:08,950 --> 00:10:11,000
Let us score the Oklo claims.

164
00:10:11,466 --> 00:10:16,756
Uranium two thirty-five depleted in the ore: evidence, confirmed.

165
00:10:16,756 --> 00:10:20,906
Fission ash like neodymium and ruthenium: evidence.

166
00:10:20,906 --> 00:10:24,405
Xenon showing a three hour cycle: evidence.

167
00:10:24,405 --> 00:10:28,799
Uranium three percent two billion years ago: evidence.

168
00:10:29,266 --> 00:10:32,956
Any tools, structures or machinery: none found.

169
00:10:32,956 --> 00:10:36,096
Designed by ancient humans: no evidence.

170
00:10:36,096 --> 00:10:38,373
Built by aliens: no evidence.

171
00:10:38,373 --> 00:10:41,200
Natural reactor: strongly supported.

172
00:10:41,666 --> 00:10:42,544
The verdict.

173
00:10:42,544 --> 00:10:43,861
Natural, but real.

174
00:10:43,861 --> 00:10:50,079
Oklo was a genuine nuclear reactor, running two billion years before the first human.

175
00:10:50,079 --> 00:10:52,566
It just needed no one to build it.

176
00:10:53,033 --> 00:10:55,174
That is the wonder of this case.

177
00:10:55,174 --> 00:10:57,850
We did not find proof of lost engineers.

178
00:10:57,850 --> 00:11:02,466
We found proof that nature can do something we thought only we could.

179
00:11:02,933 --> 00:11:12,389
Next case: a monumental gateway in the thin air of the Andes, carved from one block, tied to a city said to be impossibly old.

180
00:11:12,389 --> 00:11:14,866
Tiwanaku and the Gate of the Sun.

181
00:11:15,333 --> 00:11:16,421
What do you think?

182
00:11:16,421 --> 00:11:21,500
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
